Physics::Mechanics::Energy.b:
At equilibrium, where all stored energy is kinetic:<br>\[
 \tfrac12 kx^2 = \tfrac12 mv^2 \;\Rightarrow\; v = x\sqrt{k/m}
\]<br>Energy is conserved throughout the oscillation.

Physics::Mechanics::Momentum.b:
Momentum only (perfectly inelastic, so \(K\) is not conserved):<br>\[
 2(3) + 4(0) = (2+4)v_f \;\Rightarrow\; v_f = \mathbf{1\ m/s}
\]<br>Compare with the equal-momentum split in the elastic case: \(2\) and \(1\) m/s.

Take right as positive:<br>\[
 p_i = 1(3) + 1(-1) = 2 \;\Rightarrow\; v_f = \frac{2}{2} = \mathbf{1\ m/s}
\]<br>Momentum adds algebraically, not by averaging speeds.
